# Graph points, second batch: `facts` and `edge` to 50 cases each (60 cases).
#
# The first 20 of each are in `graph.jsonl` and `graph-build.jsonl` and stay untouched.
#
# WHY THESE TWO. They are the points the README has been shipping with a disclaimer.
# `relate_facts` says "16/20, not yet integrated", and it is not integrated *because* 16/20 on
# twenty cases is not enough to wire anything to. `verify_edge` says "15/17 when deciding" on
# twenty cases with three labels, one of which (`reversed`) had two instances. A point with two
# examples of a label has not been measured on that label, it has been sampled from it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `facts`: agree / conflict / unrelated
#
# The line that decides most of these, and the one the first batch barely tested:
#
#   agree      both can be true AND one bears on the other - a subset, a component, a
#              corroboration, a narrower or wider statement of the same quantity.
#   conflict   same attribute of the same entity, values that cannot both hold.
#   unrelated  different entities, or the same entity's different attributes, where neither
#              statement supports or contradicts the other.
#
# The trap is that `agree` and `unrelated` are BOTH "not a conflict", and the difference is
# whether one fact bears on the other. `fa-21`/`fa-31` are that pair: 1,240 homes and 312 of
# them severe is agree, because the second is a part of the first; registered in Panama and
# the director resigned in 2022 is unrelated, because neither says anything about the other.
# Five of the cases below are built on that pair and they are the ones worth looking at when
# this point scores badly.
#
# The second trap is corroboration. The same figure from a different body is `agree`, not
# `conflict` and not `unrelated`: it is the most common shape in an investigation and the
# first draft of the sibling collision point got it wrong, sending every corroboration to a
# human as if it were a contradiction.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `edge`: supported / unsupported / reversed
#
# `reversed` gets ten cases here against two before, because it is the label whose error is
# unrecoverable: a wrong-direction edge committed to a graph is read later as fact and there
# is nothing in the text to catch it. The others are cheaper to be wrong about - an
# unsupported edge is a missing edge, and a missed supported one costs a re-read.
#
# The families of `unsupported` are the ones a real extractor produces: both entities present
# in the chunk with no relation stated between them, a DIFFERENT relation stated between
# them, a relation that is merely plausible, and a relation stated about a homonym.
#
# Annotator 1 (the author) labelled every case before any decision was run.
